<section id="leave-something-useful-in-motion">
<span class="ls-essay-section-number">10</span>
<h2>
<span class="ls-stroke">Make room for the next chapter</span>
</h2>
<p>The Renaissance idea that appeals to me is a person whose interests can inform one another. A builder who reads widely. A researcher who makes things. A writer who follows a question far enough to learn a new discipline. Depth and curiosity can grow together when the cost of moving between them becomes manageable.</p>
<p>Living Documents offer one practical piece of that possibility. You close a page without abandoning its continuing responsibility. You pursue a new question without letting every previous thread become expensive to recover. When you return, the page shows what changed, what held, and what still needs you.</p>
<p>Some work should remain finished. A poem can keep its last line. A letter can belong to the day it was written. A Living Document earns its name through the responsibility it serves. We should be as free to end a routine as to begin one.</p>
<p>Choose one document you already reopen. Give it a purpose, a small job, and a result you can judge. Let it complete a cycle. Keep what helps. Revise what does not. The <a href="/manifesto/">Living Documents Manifesto</a> is the short expression of this conviction; the practice begins in a file of your own.</p>
<p>I want the knowledge we have worked to acquire to keep helping us while we discover what else we might become. The direction remains ours. Our tools can give more of our curiosity a place to go, and more of our lives room to unfold.</p>
</section>
